WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.703
NARRATOR: Imagine a product sells for one
hundred dollars and requires sixty dollars of

2
00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:06.365
variable cost for each unit.

3
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:09.432
NARRATOR: The forty-dollar difference is its
unit contribution margin.

4
00:00:09.432 --> 00:00:11.319
NARRATOR: It is not automatically profit.

5
00:00:11.319 --> 00:00:16.502
NARRATOR: It is the amount that one additional
sale contributes toward fixed costs and, after

6
00:00:16.502 --> 00:00:18.229
those are covered, operating profit.

7
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:22.244
NARRATOR: Divide forty dollars by the
one-hundred-dollar selling price and the

8
00:00:22.244 --> 00:00:24.375
contribution margin ratio is forty percent.

9
00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:29.481
NARRATOR: That means forty cents of each sales
dollar is available for fixed costs and then

10
00:00:29.481 --> 00:00:31.077
profit, under the model's assumptions.

11
00:00:31.077 --> 00:00:34.642
NARRATOR: Ratios make it easier to compare
products with different prices.

12
00:00:34.754 --> 00:00:39.349
NARRATOR: Managers use contribution margin to
explore break-even volume, product mix, and

13
00:00:39.349 --> 00:00:42.412
the effect of changing price or variable cost.

14
00:00:42.412 --> 00:00:45.514
NARRATOR: The result is only as good as the
assumptions.

15
00:00:45.514 --> 00:00:50.925
NARRATOR: Costs may change outside a relevant
range, capacity may be limited, and demand may

16
00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:52.007
respond to price.

17
00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:56.497
NARRATOR: Contribution margin is a decision
model, not a substitute for context.
